{"name":"Opal Cream","hex":"#fceece","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"opal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mineral consisting, like quartz, of silica, but inferior to quartz in hardness and specific gravity, of the chemical formula SiO2·nH2O.\n A precious gem, an iridescent gemstone found in the opal-silica mineral substrate (potch)","A precious gem, an iridescent gemstone found in the opal-silica mineral substrate (potch)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/opal"},{"term":"cream","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The butterfat or milkfat part of milk which rises to the top; this part when separated from the remainder.","The liquid separated from milk, possibly with certain other milk products added, and with at least eighteen percent of it milkfat."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Cream-coloured; having a yellowish white colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cream"}],"entries":[{"title":"Opal","blocks":[{"text":"Opal is a hydrated amorphous form of silica (SiO2·nH2O); its water content may range from 3% to 21% by weight, but is usually between 6% and 10%. Due to the amorphous (chemical) physical structure, it is classified as a mineraloid, unlike crystalline forms of silica, which are considered minerals. It is deposited at a relatively low temperature and may occur in the fissures of almost any kind of rock, being most commonly found with limonite, sandstone, rhyolite, marl, and basalt."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Opal-53714.jpg/330px-Opal-53714.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opal","wikidata":"Q171058"},{"title":"Cream","blocks":[{"text":"Cream is a dairy product composed of the higher-fat layer skimmed from the top of milk before homogenization. The butterfat, which is less dense, rises to the top and is skimmed off, resulting in gravity cream. The industrial production of cream instead uses centrifugal separators to make separator cream. Cream is often sold in grades defined by their butterfat content. It contains high levels of saturated fat, in ascending proportion from light cream to heavy cream."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/01_Mmm..._Apple_Crisp_with_Whipped_Cream.jpg/330px-01_Mmm..._Apple_Crisp_with_Whipped_Cream.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cream","wikidata":"Q13228"},{"title":"Cream (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Cream is the color of the cream produced by cattle grazing on natural pasture with plants rich in yellow carotenoid pigments, some of which are incorporated into the fresh milk. This gives a yellow tone to otherwise-white milk at higher fat concentrations. Cream is the pastel colour of yellow, much as pink is to red. By mixing yellow and white, cream can be produced."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Milk_heavy_cream_B.jpg/330px-Milk_heavy_cream_B.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cream_(colour)","wikidata":"Q2730433"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Opal_molecular_structure2.jpg/500px-Opal_molecular_structure2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Opal_molecular_structure2.jpg/1280px-Opal_molecular_structure2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Opal_molecular_structure2.jpg/500px-Opal_molecular_structure2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Precious opal consists of a regular arrangement of silica nanospheres in closely packed planes (idealized diagram).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOpal_molecular_structure2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Coober_Pedy_Opal_2.jpg/500px-Coober_Pedy_Opal_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Coober_Pedy_Opal_2.jpg/500px-Coober_Pedy_Opal_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"This precious rough opal from Coober Pedy, South Australia, displays nearly every color of the visible spectrum.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACoober_Pedy_Opal_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Common_Rough_Opal.jpg/500px-Common_Rough_Opal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Common_Rough_Opal.jpg/1280px-Common_Rough_Opal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Common_Rough_Opal.jpg/500px-Common_Rough_Opal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Common rough opal","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACommon_Rough_Opal.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/SLOVAKIAN_OPAL_12.jpg/500px-SLOVAKIAN_OPAL_12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/SLOVAKIAN_OPAL_12.jpg/1280px-SLOVAKIAN_OPAL_12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/SLOVAKIAN_OPAL_12.jpg/500px-SLOVAKIAN_OPAL_12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"White and blue opal from Slovakia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASLOVAKIAN_OPAL_12.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/co